  "verbalization": "N-methylscopolamine is shown to have a target relationship with CD276 antigen through multiple pathways, with the most direct link emerging from Path 1, which connects N-methylscopolamine to Prelamin-A/C, which in turn is targeted by a compound with a known target of CD276 antigen. Path 2 and Path 3 also establish a chain of targets leading to CD276 antigen, though with slightly lower scores. These findings suggest that N-methylscopolamine may interact with cellular components that are functionally linked to CD276 antigen, potentially through a mechanism involving the regulation of cellular processes that influence the expression or activity of CD276.\n\nThe evidence supports a possible role for N-methylscopolamine in modulating the expression or function of CD276 antigen, though the exact mechanism remains unclear. The involvement of Prelamin-A/C and other cellular targets implies that N-methylscopolamine may act through pathways that influence cellular structure or function, possibly by affecting the stability or activity of proteins involved in immune or cellular signaling. The presence of multiple targets in different pathways suggests that N-methylscopolamine may exert its effects through a complex network of interactions, potentially involving multiple cellular processes that are relevant to CD276 antigen.\n\nThe integration of these pathways highlights the potential for N-methylscopolamine to influence CD276 antigen through mechanisms that involve the regulation of cellular components, possibly by modulating the activity of proteins that are involved in immune responses or cellular homeostasis. While the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated, the evidence supports a plausible link between N-methylscopolamine and CD276 antigen, based on the observed target relationships and the functional relevance of the intermediaries involved.",
